> (The Wiki says: "Scrolls constitute 16% of all randomly-
> generated items in the main dungeon, 18% in containers".
> And the scroll of enchant weapon has this 8% probability
> of those random scrolls generated. This is quite a lot!)

So even if you had 50 chests with an average of 5 items
in each chest, you would expect to find, on average, 3.6
scrolls of enchant weapon in the whole lot.  In Gehennom
you can get more scrolls of create monster than that
per level, average, because they occur extensively in
monster inventory, which makes them common, totally
irrespective of their random object generation probability.

> If you find shops you increase the sample size, so it's
> more likely that you get the implemented distribution.

Statistically, in order to get a significantly improved
probability of getting very close to the theoretical
distribution ratios, you would have to increase the
sample size by rather a lot more than a couple of
shops can do.

If you added ten thousand shops, for example,
you would expect your percentages to get much
closer to their theoretical values.  Two or three
shops, however, would have only a negligible
impact on this.

